  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nNext week starts off with the same summer-like heat and mostly dry \nconditions due to the nearby influence of the upper-level ridge. \nHowever, the ridge will start to weaken as a trough and accompanying \nPacific cold front approaches from the west. This clash of weather \nfeatures is forecast deliver a shift from the regionally monotonous \nsummer weather, including the potential for widespread rain and \ncooler temperatures during the latter half of the week.\n\nToday, that trough is taking shape over the northern Pacific, but it \nwon't be until about mid/late tomorrow when the core of the trough \narrives on the Pacific coast and is sampled in upper-air soundings. \nModels have gradually come to a little more agreement regarding the \ntiming of the trough as it moves over the southwestern US, but there \nis still some divergence in the models regarding its behavior as it \nenters Texas that could be pivotal in assessing the mid to late week \nweather here.\n\nRegardless of timing, there is good confidence that the trough will \npull ample amounts of tropical Pacific moisture into the \nsouthwestern and central US, and this will set up an opportunity of \nwidespread rain over Texas. So far, ensemble trends in the 500mb \nheights over the western US and the progression of the trough itself \nare consistent with favoring a slower-moving trough compared to \nearlier model outputs. This has caused the most favorable period of \nrainfall for South-Central Texas to shift slightly later in the \nforecast. The window of opportunity for widespread rains still \nstretches from early Wednesday through Friday, though the highest \nchances in the long-range grand ensemble have started to center \naround Thursday. \n\nRain chances should begin before the arrival of the Pacific front as \nmoist southerly flow accelerates during its approach. However, the \nfront will act as a focus for higher rain chances. With models \nsuggesting that the trough will weaken and slow down over the \nCentral Plains late in the week, the front may be slow-moving and \nthere is some potential that it could linger over our area and \nextend rain chances into Saturday. PWATs during this period are \nforecast to increase with the GEFS, ENS, and GEPS ensembles all \nshowing mean values of around 1.8-2.1\" (near or over the 95th \npercentile), underscoring the transport of moist Pacific air. Taken \naltogether, the setup is worth monitoring for the chance of heavy \nrain near or within the region, and the WPC has started to highlight \nthe potential for rains capable of flash flooding at some point \nmid/late next week over South-Central Texas. \n\nThe sluggish nature of the trough and continued moist inflow \nsuggests we won't be seeing a sharp plunge in temperatures from the \nPacific, but the cloud cover, rain, and post-frontal air mixed in \nwill be sufficient to drop temperatures towards near normal \nterritory. The current blend of models has highs in the mid 80s to \nlow 90s late next week. However, confidence in temperatures is \nlow due to uncertainties in the frontal timing, strength, and the \ninherent variability of rain-cooled air. The drop in overnight \nlows is not expected to be as steep due to the humid air, but will\nlikely see some decrease.",
